import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const page = {
  title: "How Often Should Breeding Birds Be Screened?",
  h1: "How Often Should Breeding Birds Be Screened?",
  description: "Why there is no universal testing calendar for breeding birds, and how to build a risk-based screening schedule around introductions, breeding, shows and outbreaks.",
  canonical: "/how-often-should-breeding-birds-be-screened/",
  author: "SENO Avian Genetic Test",
  date: "November 8, 2026",
};
const articleHtml = "<p>There is no single testing interval that fits every breeding bird. A closed, stable collection and a facility that receives birds from several dealers do not share the same risk. A calendar copied from another breeder can create unnecessary testing or false reassurance.</p>\n<p>The useful question is: what events change the risk, and how much would it cost to miss an infection?</p>\n<h2>Is There a Universal Testing Schedule?</h2>\n<p>No. Testing frequency should follow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>species and known pathogen risks;</li>\n<li>bird movements and introductions;</li>\n<li>housing density and airspace;</li>\n<li>show, race, and transport exposure;</li>\n<li>previous positive results;</li>\n<li>the value of the breeding program;</li>\n<li>veterinary advice and local disease pressure.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An annual test is a reasonable baseline for some collections. It is not a complete program for a facility with continuous introductions.</p>\n<h2>Factors That Determine Screening Frequency</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Higher frequency may be justified when</th>\n<th>Lower frequency may be reasonable when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New birds</td>\n<td>Frequent introductions from unknown sources</td>\n<td>Closed collection with no new birds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Breeding</td>\n<td>New pairs, poor hatchability, young-bird losses</td>\n<td>Stable pairs with a good history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exposure</td>\n<td>Shows, races, transport, shared equipment</td>\n<td>No external contact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History</td>\n<td>Previous positive or outbreak</td>\n<td>Long negative baseline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Density</td>\n<td>Crowded housing, shared air/water</td>\n<td>Low-density, separated groups</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Testing Before Introducing New Birds</h2>\n<p>The highest-value test is usually the pre-introduction screen. A new bird should be separated while its history and test results are reviewed.</p>\n<p>Do not rely on a seller's test alone. Confirm what was tested, when, on which sample, and whether the report belongs to the bird.</p>\n<h2>Pre-Breeding Health Screening</h2>\n<p>Before pairing, review the birds that will actually breed. A physical examination and history review may be more useful than a broad panel.</p>\n<p>Testing may be considered when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a bird is new to the facility;</li>\n<li>a previous positive result exists;</li>\n<li>a pair has a history of poor hatchability or losses;</li>\n<li>a bird has clinical signs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Screening During the Breeding Season</h2>\n<p>Breeding season is not a reason to test every bird. It is a reason to pay attention to changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>young-bird losses;</li>\n<li>poor growth;</li>\n<li>respiratory signs;</li>\n<li>unusual droppings;</li>\n<li>unexpected infertility.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Targeted testing is more useful than an arbitrary calendar when a problem appears.</p>\n<h2>Periodic Monitoring of Breeding Facilities</h2>\n<p>A facility may use periodic group sampling to establish a baseline and detect trends. The value depends on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a consistent sampling plan;</li>\n<li>a consistent sample type;</li>\n<li>a consistent laboratory method;</li>\n<li>a record that can be compared over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A random sample of convenience is not trend monitoring.</p>\n<h2>Screening After Shows, Racing and Transport</h2>\n<p>Mixing events increase exposure. A bird returning from a show or race may warrant separation and observation before rejoining the group.</p>\n<p>Testing is optional and should follow the risk. A negative result does not replace quarantine.</p>\n<h2>Additional Testing During Suspected Outbreaks</h2>\n<p>When several birds develop similar signs, the priority is veterinary investigation, not a broad panel ordered without a plan.</p>\n<p>A useful outbreak response includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>separate affected birds;</li>\n<li>collect appropriate samples;</li>\n<li>test for plausible targets;</li>\n<li>trace contacts and movements;</li>\n<li>review cleaning, water, and traffic;</li>\n<li>retest before relaxing controls.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Designing a Risk-Based Testing Calendar</h2>\n<p>Build the calendar around events, not only dates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>arrival of new birds;</li>\n<li>pre-breeding review;</li>\n<li>young-bird entry;</li>\n<li>show/race return;</li>\n<li>outbreak or positive result;</li>\n<li>annual program review.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For each event, write down the decision, the test (if any), the sample, and the action threshold. A calendar without decision rules is just a reminder.</p>\n<h2>Reviewing and Updating the Program</h2>\n<p>Review the program annually and after any significant disease event. Ask:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did testing change a decision?</li>\n<li>Did we miss a problem that a different sample or target would have caught?</li>\n<li>Are we testing the same birds out of habit?</li>\n<li>Has the facility's bird movement changed?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The best program is the one that is used, not the one with the longest panel.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for selected targets. This article is a testing-strategy guide, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee that a collection is free of disease. The current menu and sample requirements should be confirmed with the laboratory and the attending veterinarian.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Should breeding birds be tested every year?</strong></p>\n<p>Not automatically. A risk-based schedule is more useful. New birds, previous positives, and disease events should drive testing more than a fixed annual rule.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I test only new birds?</strong></p>\n<p>That is a reasonable core for a closed collection, but it does not cover changes in the existing group. Clinical signs and breeding performance still matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Does a negative test mean I can skip quarantine?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A negative result covers the sample and target tested. Quarantine is a separate biosecurity layer.</p>\n<p><strong>How often should a large facility monitor?</strong></p>\n<p>There is no single number. The plan should be based on density, introductions, history, and the consequences of a missed infection.</p>\n<p><strong>Can I use the same schedule for parrots and pigeons?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Species, pathogens, housing, and movement patterns differ. Build separate risk profiles.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>WOAH — Newcastle disease. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/newcastle-disease/</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n</ol>";
